WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.792
On this slide, we are focusing on list + tuple capstone.

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:08.612
Start with the meaning of the idea before worrying about the exact syntax.

00:00:08.612 --> 00:00:14.687
Ask what information is going in, what Python is doing with it, and what result should come out.

00:00:14.687 --> 00:00:19.132
A tuple is ordered like a list but is intended to stay fixed.

00:00:19.132 --> 00:00:23.637
It works well for compact records whose positions should not change.

00:00:23.637 --> 00:00:26.777
Before clicking Run, predict the output.

00:00:26.777 --> 00:00:32.927
Then run the cell, inspect the result, and change one value or condition at a time.

00:00:32.927 --> 00:00:36.272
Rerun it and explain why the output changed.

00:00:36.272 --> 00:00:41.284
The goal is to understand the relationship between input, process, and output.
